    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nLucid Gravity Robotaxis Began Public Tests in the SF Bay Area, Can It One-Up Tesla Robotaxi?\n7 photos\nPhoto: Nuro\nIn July 2025, Lucid Motors, Nuro, and Uber signed a deal to launch a robotaxi service. Now, the three partners announced the beginning of driverless tests on public roads in the San Francisco Bay Area. The move follows months of supervised autonomous tests, with employee driverless rides planned to start soon.\nMany, and especially Tesla fans, believed that Elon Musk's company would be the only one capable of cracking autonomous driving. Even though its efforts date back more than a decade, without many achievements, they still thought others were years behind. As a Level-2 driver-assist system, the Tesla FSD (TAD in Europe) is indeed very capable. However, an autonomous driving system it is not, which Tesla makes sure you understand when you want to use it.\nIn the meantime, other carmakers have announced their own autonomous driving programs. This contradicted Tesla fans, who dreamed that all other carmakers would either license Tesla FSD or go extinct. Rivian is about to launch its point-to-point autonomous driving system later this year. Even Lucid Motors, a severely cash-strapped EV startup, is hoping to give Tesla a run for its money in autonomous driving.\nLucid Motors announced last year a partnership with Uber and Nuro to start driverless operations. Under the terms of this partnership, Lucid would provide the vehicles, Nuro would install the autonomous driving hardware and software, and Uber would operate the robotaxis. It was a surprising deal, considering Lucid Motors' difficulties, but one that could ensure its survival, thanks to the significant number of vehicles involved.\nNow, the three partners announced that they have concluded supervised autonomous driving tests and are ready to move to the next level. That is, Lucid, Nuro, and Uber have started testing Lucid Gravity robotaxis without a driver behind the wheel in the San Francisco Bay Area. Employee rides will follow shortly, with a public launch expected in the first quarter of 2027.\nPhoto: Lucid Motors Previous tests with a safety driver have been conducted in the SF Bay Area and in Houston since December 2025. These tests have paved the way for driverless trials, which are now beginning in Palo Alto, Mountain View, Los Altos, and Sunnyvale. Additional Bay Area cities will follow next year. A fleet of over 100 Lucid Gravity vehicles is involved, all equipped with Nuro's autonomous driving hardware and software.\nNuro has been mostly responsible for the tests, including safety procedures and assessments, after it received two key California permits. The CPUC Drivered Pilot Permit allows for passenger rides with a safety operator. At the same time, the California DMV Driverless Testing Permit allows Nuro to start public road testing with no driver behind the wheel. Only six companies have received the latter, and Tesla is not one of them, sadly.\nUber has committed to purchasing at least 35,000 Lucid vehicles equipped with the Nuro Driver, with plans to expand across dozens of global markets. Lucid Motors is not the only carmaker that signed such a partnership with Uber. In March, Rivian also announced a $1.25 billion deal comprising up to 50,000 R2 vehicles. It's obvious that Uber is hedging its autonomous driving bets across multiple carmakers, with Hyundai also among its partners .\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nLucid Gravity Lucid Motors Nuro Uber robotaxi autonomous driving\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT Carbon Fiber Rapier Bicycle Frame Concept Aims To Suspend the Rider and Be the Future of Cycling",
